2024-10-22 15:16:26,373 - llm.llm_use - INFO - Final LLM Answer for query 'what audits were planned in 2019? ':
The data provided represents an Internal Audit Plan for the year 2018-2019, published by the Food Standards Agency. The audit plan covers various categories of audits including Business Audits and Official Controls, as well as Local Authority Audits.

   The Official Controls audits are designed to assess the effectiveness of FSA official controls in achieving the objectives of EU law across a range of areas such as minced meat, eggs hygiene, animal welfare, and animal-by-products, among others. Some of these audits are follow-up audits intended to review the progress made in addressing issues identified during previous audits.

   The Local Authority Audits, on the other hand, focus on assessing the adequacy and effectiveness of Local Authorities (LAs) arrangements for the delivery of feed and food law enforcement services in different regions of the United Kingdom, such as Wales, Northern Ireland, and England. For instance, one audit will be conducted to evaluate the new model audits for Animal Feed Law Enforcement Services in Wales, while another will assess the organization and management of official controls delivered by LAs in Northern Ireland.

   The Business Audits category includes audits aimed at assessing FSA compliance with various standards, such as the Cabinet Office Minimum Security Standards, managing attendance policies, asset management, General Data Protection Regulation, Supplier Management for IT Help Desk Services, and Product Recall Delivery Assurance.

   In summary, these audits play a crucial role in ensuring that the Food Standards Agency is compliant with various standards, and that Local Authorities are delivering effective feed and food law enforcement services across the United Kingdom. The follow-up audits help to track progress made in addressing identified issues.
