The Food Standards Agency (FSA) is an executive agency funded by the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (Defra) in the United Kingdom. Its primary role is to ensure that food and feed are safe and that food standards are effectively managed. The FSA works to protect public health through its regulatory functions concerning food safety and standards, ensuring animals are slaughtered humanely, promoting healthy diets, safeguarding against food fraud, and maintaining the integrity of British foods.

In 2017, as detailed in the dataset titled "Internal Audit Plan" available [here](https://fsadata.github.io/internal-audit-plan/data/internal-audit-plan-2017-18.csv), the FSA planned several internal audits across various categories including Business Audits and Official Controls Audits. These audits aimed at evaluating operational efficiency, risk management strategies (such as planning for Brexit), compliance with policies on open data usage and transparency, strategic investments, performance management systems, contract management processes for services and goods supply, and more.

The FSA's focus on these areas of audit reflects its commitment to continuous improvement in managing risks related to food safety and standards. Through these audits, the agency ensures that it can meet its objectives effectively while also identifying opportunities for enhancing operational efficiency, service delivery, resource utilization, and strategic planning capabilities. 

For a comprehensive understanding or further details about FSA's role and functions, one might refer to their official website at [foodstandards.gov.uk](https://www.foodstandards.gov.uk/).
